## Why This Is Asked
Assesses ability to design distributed workflow using sagas, CQRS, and event-sourcing in real-world microservices; probes handling of failure modes, idempotency, compensations, and consistency guarantees.

## Key Concepts
- Saga orchestration vs choreography
- Event store and projections for CQRS
- Compensating actions and timeouts
- Idempotent handlers and outbox pattern

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code: saga orchestrator pattern
class CheckoutSaga {
  async start(order) {
    await dispatch('ReserveInventory', order);
  }
  async onEvent(event) {
    if (event.type === 'InventoryReserved') await dispatch('CapturePayment', event.order);
    else if (event.type === 'PaymentCaptured') await dispatch('ScheduleShipment', event.order);
    else if (event.type === 'ShipmentScheduled') complete(event.order);
    else if (event.type.endsWith('Failed')) compensate(event.order);
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- What are the trade-offs between orchestration and choreography in this scenario?
- How would you test the saga under partial failures and timeouts?

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes cross-protocol data governance: masking at the boundary across REST, GraphQL, and gRPC, driven by per-tenant OpenAPI contracts. It tests how to derive and enforce field visibility, roll out changes safely, and audit policy drift.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant field-level visibility across protocols
- Contract-driven masking rules derived from OpenAPI
- Central registry (Redis) for tenant/resource visibility
- Canary rollouts and rollout safety across services

## Code Example

```javascript
function maskVisible(obj, tenantId, contract) {
  if (Array.isArray(obj)) return obj.map(v => maskVisible(v, tenantId, contract));
  if (obj === null || typeof obj !== 'object') return obj;
  const out = {};
  const allowed = contract?.visibleFields || [];
  for (const [k, v] of Object.entries(obj)) {
    out[k] = allowed.includes(k) ? v : 'REDACTED';
  }
  return out;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test contract drift and automated rollback when masking changes break clients?
- What metrics would you collect to detect masking-related regressions across REST, GraphQL, and gRPC?

